>
  <p class="description scribble-target">The mechanics of link acquisition have shifted. Mass outreach, templated pitches, and link exchanges still exist — but their yield is approaching zero. What works now is narrower, slower, and more durable.</p>

  <h2 class="note-heading">Why Most Outreach Fails</h2>
  <p class="note-text">Google's Penguin update taught the industry to fear spammy links. What it didn't teach was how to replace volume with quality. Most agencies responded by doing the same thing more carefully — templated emails, small personalisation tokens, domain authority thresholds. The result is a market saturated with nearly identical outreach that editors have learned to filter instantly.</p>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>Response rates on cold link outreach have dropped below 2% at scale. The cost per acquired link has risen accordingly.</li>
    <li>AI-generated outreach is now identifiable at a glance. Editors flag it immediately and many domains have added it to their spam filters.</li>
    <li>Sites that rely on purchased or exchanged links face compounding risk as Google's spam systems improve quarterly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">What Moves Rankings Now</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li><strong>Original data and research</strong> — proprietary datasets, surveys, and studies attract links that cannot be manufactured. A single well-distributed data piece can generate more authoritative links than a year of outreach.</li>
    <li><strong>Tool-based link magnets</strong> — free calculators, graders, and diagnostic tools embedded on your domain accumulate links passively over time as practitioners share them.</li>
    <li><strong>Earned media through positioning</strong> — commentary, predictions, and counter-arguments placed in trade publications. Requires genuine expertise and a clear point of view.</li>
    <li><strong>Digital PR with news hooks</strong> — timely stories tied to industry data or trend analysis picked up by journalists with genuine editorial standards.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">The Role of AI Summaries</h2>
  <p class="note-text">Google's AI Overviews cite sources. A link from a cited source carries compounding value — both as a traditional ranking signal and as a source signal for generative answers. Optimising for citation means producing content that is clear, well-structured, and factually verifiable. The criteria overlap almost entirely with good editorial standards.</p>

  <h2 class="note-heading">A Practical Acquisition Framework</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>Audit existing unlinked mentions using tools like Ahrefs Alerts or Brand24. These are the easiest links to convert — a polite email referencing the mention converts at 15–30%.</li>
    <li>Build one significant original asset per quarter. Invest the resources you would have spent on a hundred outreach emails into one piece of research worth linking to.</li>
    <li>Identify three to five publications in your vertical where a byline or quoted expert comment would reach the right audience. Build a relationship before asking for anything.</li>
    <li>Track topical authority gains, not just link counts. A rising topic cluster score in tools like Semrush or Sistrix is a more reliable leading indicator than domain rating.</li>
  </ul>
